  "task_id": "LEV-CH01-ACOUSTICS-EIGENVALUES-CANONICAL-20260908",
  "trigger": [
    "judge classifications differ",
    "round-trip judge requested adjudication",
    "round-trip judge classification is undetermined",
    "round-trip judge left semantic_checklist item C04 unclear",
    "round-trip judge left semantic_checklist item C10 unclear",
    "round-trip judge left semantic_checklist item C11 unclear",
    "round-trip judge left implication translation_implies_source unclear"
  ],
  "resolved_items": [
    {
      "item": "judge classifications differ",
      "resolution": "The appropriate classification is faithful-equivalent in the inherited physical material context. The selected claim concerns the acoustic matrix of a material with density and sound speed, rather than a universally quantified algebraic assertion for every real pair admitting a reciprocal and square root. The explicit eigenvectors and traveling-mode conclusions are derivable realizations of that claim, not independent logical strengthening.",
      "primary_evidence": "The attached printed page 2 introduces acoustic waves in gas or an elastic solid, describes K and ρ as material parameters, displays A = [[0,K],[1/ρ,0]] in (1.6), and defines c = √(K/ρ). Printed page 3 identifies the two eigenvalues and their acoustic propagation meaning. The elaborated target preserves this matrix, this c, and both signed modes."
    },
    {
      "item": "round-trip judge requested adjudication",
      "resolution": "The requested parameter-domain review is resolved through the source's physical object roles. The round-trip judge correctly observes that reciprocal existence and positive K/ρ alone do not establish individual positivity. The missing premise comes from ρ being the density of the gas or solid carrying the acoustic waves. In this ordinary material setting, density is nonnegative, and the displayed reciprocal excludes zero. Thus ρ > 0; the positive sound speed then gives K = ρc² > 0. This is inherited contextual interpretation, not an explicitly printed pair of inequalities.",
      "primary_evidence": "Printed page 2 begins with propagation through gas or solid, explicitly discusses rightward sound propagation with c > 0, and identifies ρ as density in the paragraph following (1.5). It subsequently identifies √(K/ρ) as the speed of sound. The target's only outer side conditions are K > 0 and ρ > 0."
    },
    {
      "item": "round-trip judge classification is undetermined",
      "resolution": "No unresolved declaration meaning changes the result. Fresh expansion confirms the blind translation. D001 requires actual time and space derivatives and their zero matrix residual; D002 and D007 construct f(x−st)v; D003 and D005 construct the column vectors (−ρc,1) and (ρc,1). The reused D004 body, independently available in the complete inventory, has exactly the source matrix entries. The remaining reused operations preserve multiplication, nonzeroness, dimension, reciprocal, and coordinate order. With the domain resolved contextually, both implications hold.",
      "primary_evidence": "D004 uses nested vecCons with rows (0,K) and (ρ⁻¹,0). D071, D073–D075, and D082 expose the identity matrix conversion; D083–D084 preserve constructor order; D070 fixes dimension two. D020 and D066 select real multiplication; D029 is negated equality; D080 and D094 select real inverse. D027 is row-dot-vector multiplication. These supplied bodies, rather than their reuse hashes, establish their effects."
    },
    {
      "item": "round-trip judge left semantic_checklist item C04 unclear",
      "resolution": "C04 passes under the inherited physical interpretation. Positive density and positive sound speed justify the parameter hypotheses, without attributing explicit inequalities to the passage. The profile derivative conditions occur only inside the separate traveling-mode implications. They do not restrict either eigenvalue assertion and are the appropriate local regularity conditions for the classical PDE predicate.",
      "primary_evidence": "Printed pages 2–3 describe opposite acoustic propagation directions at nonzero sound speed. The fully elaborated target places both eigenvector equations outside the HasDerivAt implications. D023 uses ordinary at-point differentiation; D001 existentially supplies actual derivatives of the same field. D006 and the supplied real and product-topology instances add no profile or material assumption."
    },
    {
      "item": "round-trip judge left semantic_checklist item C10 unclear",
      "resolution": "C10 passes. Dimension two, real scalars, and constant parameters exactly match the selected example. Simultaneously negative K and ρ can satisfy the analogous algebraic eigenvalue identities, but negative material density is not an instance of the source's gas-or-solid acoustic example. Excluding that algebraic extension does not reduce the applicability of the selected physical claim. The positivity premises are not counted as stronger conclusions.",
      "primary_evidence": "Printed page 1 specifies real constant matrices; printed page 2 fixes q = [p,u]ᵀ and the material density and bulk modulus. The target uses Real parameters and Matrix (Fin 2) (Fin 2) Real. Its chosen vectors merely normalize the velocity component to one."
    },
    {
      "item": "round-trip judge left semantic_checklist item C11 unclear",
      "resolution": "C11 passes for the selected physical context. Zero density makes the displayed ordinary reciprocal undefined. Zero sound speed does not describe the source's two oppositely propagating acoustic waves, and a negative radicand does not give its real sound speed. The target consistently excludes these cases. Its hypotheses are satisfiable, and neither empty indices, zero-vector witnesses, nor impossible derivative premises make the result vacuous.",
      "primary_evidence": "Printed page 2 displays 1/ρ and c = √(K/ρ), identifies positive and negative propagation directions, and printed page 3 describes distinct acoustic waves. D059's square-root truncation and D094's totalized inverse are inactive when K,ρ > 0. For K = ρ = 1, c = 1 and the vectors are (−1,1) and (1,1); the profile f(z) = z has derivative 1 everywhere and produces nonconstant traveling states."
    },
    {
      "item": "round-trip judge left implication translation_implies_source unclear",
      "resolution": "The implication is yes in the inherited material context. Both nonzero column-vector equations establish the source eigenvalues. Their distinctness and dimension two establish spectral completeness. The translated fields solve the exact acoustic system and move at the asserted signed speeds, preserving the propagation interpretation. The converse follows by normalizing eigenvectors of the displayed matrix and applying ordinary differentiation; no additional independent result or restricted spectral premise is introduced.",
      "primary_evidence": "For c² = K/ρ, A(−ρc,1) = (K,−c) = −c(−ρc,1) and A(ρc,1) = (K,c) = c(ρc,1). The determinant of the two columns is −2ρc ≠ 0. D002 and D007 give q(x,t) = f(x−st)v; D001 requires q_t + A q_x = 0. With f′(x−st) = d, the derivative values are q_t = −sdv and q_x = dv, so the residual is d(Av−sv) = 0."
    }
  ],
  "implications": {
    "lean_implies_source": {
      "verdict": "yes",
      "reasoning": "Interpret the source in its stated gas-or-solid material setting: ρ is positive material density and c is the positive sound speed, so K = ρc² is positive. The target therefore applies throughout this inherited setting. Its matrix is exactly (1.6), and its locally defined speed is exactly √(K/ρ). Nonzero vectors satisfy Av₋ = −cv₋ and Av₊ = cv₊. Since c > 0, these are two distinct eigenvalues and exhaust the spectrum in dimension two. The fields f(x+ct)v₋ and f(x−ct)v₊ satisfy the same acoustic PDE wherever the profile is differentiable, and their phases encode leftward and rightward translation. Thus both the spectral identification and its wave-speed meaning are represented."
    },
    "source_implies_lean": {
      "verdict": "yes",
      "reasoning": "For any positive K and ρ in the target domain, the source definition gives c > 0 and K = ρc². Its displayed matrix has the explicit eigenvectors v₋ = (−ρc,1) and v₊ = (ρc,1), as verified by direct multiplication; their second coordinate ensures nonzeroness. Equivalently, any nonzero eigenvector for either signed eigenvalue can be normalized to this form: its velocity coordinate cannot vanish, since the second row would then force its pressure coordinate to vanish. For either branch, a derivative d of f at x−st supplies genuine partial derivatives −sdv and dv of f(x−st)v. Their matrix residual is zero. The independent universal profile and point binders and the existential derivative witnesses consequently follow from the source objects by ordinary algebra and calculus."
    }
  },
  "classification": "faithful-equivalent",
  "accepted": true,
  "remaining_uncertainties": [],
  "findings": [
    {
      "severity": "note",
      "category": "inherited-physical-domain",
      "evidence": "The source identifies ρ as material density and c as the sound speed of waves propagating in opposite directions; it does not explicitly print K > 0 and ρ > 0. Reciprocal existence and positive K/ρ alone would also allow simultaneously negative parameters.",
      "impact": "Acceptance uses the physical roles and surrounding propagation context as inherited assumptions. It does not certify equivalence to a broader algebraic assertion for arbitrary signed parameters or degenerate sound speeds."
    },
    {
      "severity": "note",
      "category": "eigenvector-orientation",
      "evidence": "D003 is the column vector (−ρc,1), and the target applies A.mulVec to it. It is not a row eigenvector despite the local declaration's use of the word Left. The source's characteristic covectors are (1,ρc) and (1,−ρc).",
      "impact": "There is no semantic orientation error. For the negative mode, w¹ vanishes and w² = −2ρc f(x+ct); for the positive mode, w² vanishes and w¹ = 2ρc f(x−ct). The source's direction assignments are preserved."
    },
    {
      "severity": "note",
      "category": "derived-conclusions-and-nonvacuity",
      "evidence": "The target supplies explicit nonzero eigenvectors and pointwise classical traveling-mode solutions. These follow from the displayed matrix and sound-speed relation, and K = ρ = 1 with f(z) = z gives a nontrivial instance.",
      "impact": "The extra explicit witnesses do not warrant faithful-stronger classification. They express the selected spectral and propagation content with ordinary derivative existence and without vacuous premises."
    }
  ],
  "rationale": "Independent inspection of the attached primary-source page images and the inline declaration evidence resolves the disagreement through inherited physical context, not majority vote. The round-trip objection correctly distinguishes algebraic sign conditions from individual positivity, but the source additionally fixes density as a physical material quantity and describes positive sound propagation. Those roles justify the target domain. Fresh checks of the definitions, including reused declarations, confirm the exact matrix, quotient, column-vector action, derivative semantics, and propagation signs. Both implication directions hold; the explicit witnesses are derivable, and the theorem has nontrivial instances. No tools or target proof were used, and no source or rendering hashes were independently recomputed."
}
